---
import Breadcrumbs from "@typeshade/site-ui/astro/Breadcrumbs";
import PageHeading from "@typeshade/site-ui/astro/PageHeading";
import TextLink from "@typeshade/site-ui/astro/TextLink";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
import ReadingSurface from "@typeshade/site-ui/astro/ReadingSurface";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import InlineTypeScript from "@typeshade/site-ui/astro/InlineTypeScript";
const code = `const input = device.tensor(values, { shape: [batch, features] });
const logits = input.matmul(weights).add(bias);
const output = logits.relu();

// This is the materialization boundary.
const result = await output.data();`;
---

<SiteLayout
  locale="en"
  active="execution"
  title="TypeNN execution model"
  description="How a Tensor graph runs its TypeShade kernel functions when you call data()."
>
  <ReadingSurface class="nn-docs">
    <article class="docs-article">
      <Breadcrumbs aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">TypeNN</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <a href="/guide/">Guides</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <span>Execution model</span>
      </Breadcrumbs>
      <PageHeading>
        <Fragment slot="eyebrow">Guides</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="title">Execution model</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="description">
          TypeNN builds and schedules Tensor graphs. TypeShade compiles their
          kernel functions and runs them on the selected backend.
        </Fragment>
      </PageHeading>
      <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Tensor calls build a deferred graph</h2>
        <p>
          Operations such as <InlineTypeScript code="matmul()" />,{" "}
          <InlineTypeScript code="add()" />, and <InlineTypeScript
            code="relu()"
          /> do not compute values immediately. Each one records a node that holds
          a TypeShade kernel function, the input Tensors, the scalars the kernel
          takes by value, and the gradient rule.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock title="A small Tensor graph" file="model.ts" code={code} />
      </section>
      <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>How data() computes a Tensor result</h2>
        <p>
          When you call <InlineTypeScript code="data()" /> for a Tensor without
          a computed array, TypeNN collects the graph nodes needed to produce
          it. It creates an output array for each node and uploads the leaf
          arrays. TypeNN then calls TypeShade <InlineTypeScript code="fuse()" />
          once, running each node's kernel in graph order. The compiler groups
          compatible calls into dispatches, and TypeNN reads the requested
          Tensor once.
        </p>
        <div class="execution-lanes">
          <div>
            <span class="lane-label">TypeNN</span>
            <p>Shape checks · graph nodes · autograd · kernel functions</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="lane-label">TypeShade</span>
            <p>
              compiler dispatch plan · backend tiers · runtime resources
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Backends and limits</h2>
        <div class="backend-table" role="table" aria-label="Backend support">
          <div class="table-row table-head" role="row">
            <span role="columnheader">Tier</span>
            <span role="columnheader">Selection</span>
            <span role="columnheader">Current note</span>
          </div>
          <div class="table-row" role="row">
            <strong role="cell">CPU</strong>
            <span role="cell">Auto fallback or explicit</span>
            <span role="cell">
              Runs the same kernel functions, one call after the other. Suited
              to correctness and fallback.
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="table-row" role="row">
            <strong role="cell">WebGPU</strong>
            <span role="cell">Auto or explicit</span>
            <span role="cell">
              Native GPU compute when the browser exposes an adapter.
              Elementwise chains fuse into one dispatch.
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="table-row" role="row">
            <strong role="cell">WebGL2</strong>
            <span role="cell">Explicit</span>
            <span role="cell">
              Runs the same kernel functions apart, one call after the other. A
              call it cannot run goes to the CPU tier.
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="fine-print">
          TypeShade uses one call-layer configuration for the whole process,
          so each JavaScript context supports one active TypeNN Device.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Read the plan in device.lastReport</h2>
        <p>
          The compiler puts consecutive kernel calls in one dispatch when
          TypeShade's fusion rules hold. On WebGPU,{" "}
          <InlineTypeScript code="a.add(b).mul(c).relu()" /> runs as one dispatch. A reduction
          with a different output count, a read at another index, or too many
          storage buffers starts a new dispatch. After a read,
          <code>device.lastReport</code> shows the tier,
          the dispatches, the submissions, and the groups of calls. On the CPU
          tier the report has no dispatches and one group for each call.
        </p>
        <p>
          <TextLink href="https://github.com/typeshade/typenn#how-a-read-runs">
            Read how a read runs ↗
          </TextLink>
        </p>
      </section>
      <nav class="doc-pagination">
        <a href="/guide/">
          <small>Previous</small>
          <strong>Get started</strong>
        </a>
        <a href="/guide/training/">
          <small>Next</small>
          <strong>Training</strong>
          <span>→</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </ReadingSurface>
</SiteLayout>
